A||||17||Gold-86SE||Rear Calipers||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||12-07-2000||02:20 PM||||<BR>Before I did my engine change my rear brakes on my 84 worked fine.  Now after my engine change things, don't work so well.<P>The car has only been down about a month.  The calipers were removed from the rotor and hung by the chemny with care.  No opening of the brake line was made to remove the cradle.<P>When placing the brakes back togehter, I found that I could not callapse the calipers, either one.  And learned that I was trying to callapse the caliper using the wrong method. So, I changed methods and the calipers did callapse with some concerded effort.  Put everything back together, and my brakes are dragging.  Whew! They stink!!<P>Additionally, I have slack in my hand brake and I cannot even get the thing to pull tight when trying to apply the e-brake.<P>What have I done to cause my problem? Or, are they gone and need replacement?<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-07-2000||02:40 PM||||How did you end up collapsing the pistons? sounds like you created a bind on the inside of the calipers resulting a situation of E-brake applied.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||12-07-2000||03:12 PM||||<BR>I removed the e-brake lever and used a c-clamp to collapse the caliper.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-07-2000||08:21 PM||||did you make sure that the shaft the e-brake lever came off of was able to turn freely?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||12-08-2000||09:22 AM||||<BR>Indiana, I'm not sure my processor is understanding your input.<P>When I removed the e-brake lever, I placed the top of the C-clamp to the side of the e-brake lever bolt, and collapsed the caliper.<P>The e-brake lever has a six-point hole, matching the shape of the bolt, so I placed the lever back on the brake trying to align it and I am assuming that the lever fit properly.  Also, the e-brake lever, at the time of installation, was able to rotate down mimicing the action performed by the e-brake cable.<P>Hope this information helps.<P>Additionally, if I have done something to my brakes, it would be pointless to purchase new, unless I destroyed them somehow. But if it is the brakes problem then new ones are in order.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||12-08-2000||10:23 AM||||i've got my own queston about these rear brake calipers.<P>how do you get the "inner" pad to align up with the knuckle, so it all fits correctly?<P>i spent a hour trying to get this to happen last night.  i have new calipers, pads, and rotors and cant seem to get them to both fit on.<P>it looks to me that the "inner" pad needs to be placed on the rotor 1st then slike the caliper over it.  i may be wrong.  but if that's the case, i will need to colapse the piston more (than factory setting) so that i can do this.  what is the <B>best</B> way to do so?<P>thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zorba||12-08-2000||11:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>i've got my own queston about these rear brake calipers.<P>how do you  hoop</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I am no mechanic but I just installed my rear calipers with ease. I bought them new from Checkers. After installing all the bushings and sleeves, it was easy to do.<P>However, there is a metal ring that you have to install on the piston face, this round metal ring holds the back (inner) pad in place. Then you slide the outter pad in. Easy to do. With the factory settings, the way they come from the store, I had no need to press the piston in further.<BR>A key element on this installation is making sure that the BLEEDER SCREW is on TOP of the caliper on both sides. I was not doing this correctly before and could not get the calipers to fit because I wasn't paying attention about the position of the bleeder screw and was trying to install the left caliper on the right side and vice versa.<BR>Once you slide the caliper over the rotor and align the mounting bolts, it's done. Tighten to proper torque. Someone suggested to fill the calipers with brake fluid BEFORE installation but I found this unnecessary. Bleeding took about 5 minutes for each side.<BR>I am not sure how to install my hand brake cables and brackets the correct way though but I will try to find additional info on this subject. I hope this helps.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||12-08-2000||02:43 PM||||Well, the 'proper' way to get the pistons back into the caliper body is by turning them back down. This procedure may aslo be accomplished by turning the e-brake screw to 'pull' the piston back. A combination of the two is also acceptable. It is possible that since the pistons were forced back into their bores that the internal spring has been 'pre-loaded' and is providing a constant pressure to the pads. It may be possible to correct this condition while the calipers are still installed on the car. Either jack up and support the rear of car (tire on or off, your preference) and removing the e-brake lever then backing the lever action screw off some to relieve the 'pre-load' tension taking car to make sure that the shaft seal remains intact and in place prior to re-assembly. <P>BHooper - to get the indendations to line up with the pads knobs (I call them something else that the system won't like LOL), simply turn them into position. You need the ones that are a closed indent I think (I have to measure every time to be sure!) to line up with the pads. Since you have 'new' calipers this should pose no problem. Then install the keeper clip to the top of piston and rotate it into place and install the inner pad.<BR>If you need further explaination, send mail!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||12-08-2000||03:00 PM||||Indiana, this statement is not intended to flame, but before I posted this question I did a search and found this thread: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004214.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004214.html</A> <P>Much discussion was made regarding the calipers and theogre stated   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You DO NOT rotate stock 84-87 Fiero rear pistons to retract them.<P>The correct procedure is to remove the e brake arm, cover the e brake stud with a socket and press in the piston with a clamp. The stud will rotate under the socket as the piston is pressed in. This is the procedure listed in Every Fiero service book I can find including GM's.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I used this method, except that I did not place a socket over the e-brake stud, I set the clamp to the side of the e-brake stud.  However, I will say this I never noticed the stud turn during compression of the piston.<P>With all this I am still lost, I guess I'll have to tear apart again tonight.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mark||12-08-2000||06:23 PM||||You cannot "pull" the piston in by rotating the E-brake screw.  What will most likely happen is the E-brake screw will get pulled into the caliper as it threads into the piston (this also causes fluid to leak out past the seal).  The E-brake screw is designed to push the piston out of the bore as it bears against the back of the caliper.  I suggest using the C-clamp and socket method. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||12-08-2000||08:25 PM||||Gold-86SE<P>If you would care to look into a Chiltons Repair Manual, part # 7571 copyright 1989 on page 288, in the 'TO INSTALL' procedure.<BR>Step 1 clearly states (and I quote)<P>Bottom the piston into the caliper bore using tool J-36621. Turn the LEFT piston in a counterclockwise direction and the RIGHT piston in a clockwise direction to move the piston back into the bore. The piston MUST be bottomed in the bore to install new brake pads.<P>This procedure duplicates itself on the adjacent page for the '88 model year as well.<P>The Haynes manual contradicts itself in several areas in the brake sections, espically in the caliper overhaul section. While both methods work, turning the piston in does work the best as I have found. I do maintain ALL my own vehicles to include the Fieros on the black top of my own. They are listed in my profile if you haven't looked. Actually I don't think that they're all listed, I own and operate 14 - at least one of each year made.<BR>Being a former certified drive train and suspension mechanic (also A&P) You didn't pull any flames what-so-ever.<P>I would also like to add that I have knowledge of the rear of the casting being broken out using C-clamps in cases of moisture being present in the caliper. Unfortunatly this isn't discovered untill it's too late. If it doesn't want to move freely as it should I do coax it while turning the e-brake screw in conjunction with the piston to bottom. If it's still too tuff to turn, I either rebuild or replace them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||12-08-2000||10:42 PM||||i understand what you are saying.  but my calipers are new, and have no moisture in them.  i would think, if i'm carefull, i should be ok with the c clamp.  <P>i only need to move them about 1/8" or so.<P>thanks!<P>hoop||||reg||2
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||12-09-2000||10:23 AM||||Mark,<P>I did forget to say that when 'pulling' the piston back into the bore a large flat washer  with a nut and jamb nut are added to the shaft end to 'pull' the piston back.<BR>Obviously you've never had one apart, I would think that an engineer would have thought of the obvious.<P><BR>Go here.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||-=shame=-||12-09-2000||12:42 PM||||Engineers don't think that way.||||reg||1
