Z||000016||Gold-86SE||12-11-2000||09:43 AM||||<BR>Okay guys. Problem solved. Sorry for the delay, but I could not get to the car Friday night or Saturday, so I did it at 10pm last night. Here's the story.<P>Lifted the car, saddled her on stands, went to the tool box and what is it that I see on the work bence? Springs. Springs I tell you. AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>Just ask my wife, I went nutso.  I forgot to put the springs on the e-brake cable.  No wonder the things are dragging.  That is what I get for trying to finish a project at 1:30 in the morning on a school night. Oh, well I'll know better next time.<P>BTW, when working on your rear calipers, and you use the e-brake, don't forget to replace the e-brake spring. Or you too will be saying, "AAAAAAAAAAAAHHHHHHHHHHH! DIRTY WORD! DIRTY WORD! DIRTY WORD! DIRTY WORD! "<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||12-11-2000||10:22 AM||||i also got my calipers installed, sunday.<P>I borrowed a tool to compress the piston.  However, not having a vice, or a decent workbench, made that a pain.  So I removed the ebrake bracket, and used a c-clamp.<P>Then, I slotted the caliper on the lower part of the knuckle, and used a screw driver to position the inner pad into place.  Then all it took was a few "love taps" with the rubber mallet, to get the caliper positioned<BR>properly.<P>All appears well.  But of course, I won't know until I get it on the road... sometime Wednesday night.  (I am still waiting for the KYB shocks and struts)<P>Tonight, I hope to get the e-brakes hooked up, might even install the new cables i have (or not).<P>hoop||||reg||1
